    body: `
<p>English puts "to" after "want" and "need", and has odd rules for "can", "must" and "may". Indonesian is simpler: a helping word goes straight before the verb, and nothing else changes.</p>
<h3>Six helping words</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Indonesian</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">mau</td><td>want to (everyday)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ingin</td><td>want to, wish to (a little more careful)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">bisa</td><td>can, be able to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">harus</td><td>must, have to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">boleh</td><td>may, be allowed to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">perlu</td><td>need to</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The helping word is the same for "I", "you" and "she", and it does not change for the past or the future either. When time matters, a time word goes in front: <b>Saya sudah bisa berenang</b> means "I can swim now".</p>
{{ex:Saya harus pergi sekarang.|SAH-yah HAH-roos puhr-GHEE suh-KAH-rahng|I have to go now.}}
{{ex:Dia perlu istirahat.|DEE-ah puhr-LOO ees-tee-RAH-haht|She needs to rest.}}
<h3>No word for "to"</h3>
<p>English says "I want <i>to</i> eat". Indonesian has nothing in that spot: <b>Saya mau makan</b>. Adding <b>untuk</b> ("for, in order to") is a calque, a word-for-word copy from English.</p>
{{ex:Saya mau makan.|SAH-yah mow MAH-kahn|I want to eat.}}
{{ex:Saya ingin belajar bahasa Indonesia.|SAH-yah EE-ngeen buh-LAH-jahr bah-HAH-sah een-doh-neh-SEE-ah|I want to learn Indonesian.}}
<h3>bisa: can, and "speak"</h3>
<p><b>bisa</b> is about being able to. With a language it means "speak": <b>Bisa bahasa Inggris?</b> is "Do you speak English?". In writing you will also see <b>dapat</b>.</p>
{{ex:Saya tidak bisa berenang.|SAH-yah TEE-dahq BEE-sah buh-ruh-NAHNG|I can't swim.}}
{{ex:Ibu bisa bahasa Inggris?|EE-boo BEE-sah bah-HAH-sah EENG-grees|Do you speak English? (to a woman)}}
<h3>boleh: may, allowed</h3>
<p><b>boleh</b> is about permission, not ability. To ask "May I…?", start with <b>Boleh saya</b>. English speakers often say "Can I…?" for permission; in Indonesian, think "Am I allowed?" and use <b>boleh</b>. The short answers are <b>Boleh</b> ("Sure, go ahead") and <b>Tidak boleh</b> ("That's not allowed").</p>
{{ex:Boleh saya duduk di sini?|BOH-lehh SAH-yah DOO-dooq dee SEE-nee|May I sit here?}}
{{ex:Di sini tidak boleh merokok.|dee SEE-nee TEE-dahq BOH-lehh muh-ROH-kohq|No smoking here.}}
<h3>Saying no</h3>
<p><b>tidak</b> goes in front of the helping word: <b>tidak bisa</b> (can't), <b>tidak boleh</b> (not allowed), <b>tidak perlu</b> (no need to).</p>
{{ex:Bapak tidak perlu membayar.|BAH-pahq TEE-dahq puhr-LOO muhm-BAH-yahr|You don't need to pay. (to a man)}}
<h3>Asking for help</h3>
<p><b>Bisa tolong</b> + verb is a polite "Could you…?". The next lesson is all about <b>tolong</b>.</p>
{{ex:Bisa tolong bantu saya, Pak?|BEE-sah TOH-lohng BAHN-too SAH-yah pahq|Could you help me, sir?}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> copying the English "to". <b>Saya mau untuk pergi</b> → <b>Saya mau pergi</b>.</div>
`,
