    id: "passive",
    title: "The di- passive",
    blurb: "Say what is done or was done to something, the way Indonesians do it every day: Kamarnya sudah dipesan.",
    deck: "Transport and travel",
    body: `
<p>English says "The room has been booked". Indonesian builds the same idea with the prefix <b>di-</b>: <b>Kamarnya sudah dipesan</b>. The thing comes first, then the <b>di-</b> verb.</p>
<h3>Making a di- verb</h3>
<p>Take a <b>meN-</b> verb, take off <b>meN-</b> to get the root back, and put <b>di-</b> on the root. Any letter that dropped after <b>meN-</b> comes back: <b>menulis</b> lost its t, and <b>ditulis</b> has it again. Only verbs that take an object, the <b>meN-</b> kind, have a <b>di-</b> form.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>meN- verb</th><th>Root</th><th>di- verb</th></tr><tr><td class="k">memesan</td><td class="k">pesan</td><td class="k">dipesan</td></tr><tr><td class="k">membeli</td><td class="k">beli</td><td class="k">dibeli</td></tr><tr><td class="k">menulis</td><td class="k">tulis</td><td class="k">ditulis</td></tr><tr><td class="k">mengirim</td><td class="k">kirim</td><td class="k">dikirim</td></tr><tr><td class="k">mencuci</td><td class="k">cuci</td><td class="k">dicuci</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Kamarnya sudah dipesan.|kah-MAHR-nyah SOO-dahh dee-puh-SAHN|The room has been booked.}}
{{ex:Tiketnya bisa dibeli di stasiun.|tee-KEHT-nyah BEE-sah dee-buh-LEE dee stah-SEE-oon|Tickets can be bought at the station.}}
<h3>Time words still do the work</h3>
<p>English changes "is", "was" and "will be". Indonesian keeps the <b>di-</b> verb as it is and adds a time word when time matters: <b>sudah dipesan</b>, <b>akan dikirim</b>.</p>
{{ex:Suratnya akan dikirim besok.|soo-RAHT-nyah AH-kahn dee-KEE-reem BEH-sohq|The letter will be sent tomorrow.}}
<h3>"By": oleh</h3>
<p>To say who did it, add <b>oleh</b> ("by"). In speech, <b>oleh</b> is often left out when the person comes right after the verb.</p>
{{ex:Surat ini ditulis oleh Pak Budi.|SOO-raht EE-nee dee-TOO-lees OH-lehh pahq BOO-dee|This letter was written by Mr. Budi.}}
{{ex:Nasi goreng ini dimasak ibu saya.|NAH-see GOH-rehng EE-nee dee-MAH-sahq EE-boo SAH-yah|This fried rice was cooked by my mother.}}
<h3>Indonesians like the passive</h3>
<p>English uses the passive mostly in formal writing. Indonesian uses it all the time in speech, often where English would say "you" or "they": "Where do you buy tickets?" is naturally <b>Tiketnya dibeli di mana?</b>. Using it will make you sound more natural, not more formal.</p>
{{ex:Makanannya sudah dibayar.|mah-kah-NAHN-nyah SOO-dahh dee-BAH-yahr|The food has already been paid for.}}
<h3>One word or two?</h3>
<p>The <b>di-</b> of a verb is written joined: <b>dimakan</b>. The word <b>di</b> before a place ("at, in") is written apart: <b>di rumah</b>. The same spelling rule applies in every sentence.</p>
{{ex:Kuenya dimakan di rumah.|koo-EH-nyah dee-MAH-kahn dee ROO-mahh|The cake was eaten at home.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> mixing up the two kinds of di. <b>di makan dirumah</b> → <b>dimakan di rumah</b>.</div>
`,
