    id: "compare",
    title: "lebih, paling: comparing",
    blurb: "Compare prices and things: cheaper, the cheapest, the same size, too expensive.",
    deck: "Shopping and bargaining",
    body: `
<p>English changes the describing word itself: cheap, cheap<i>er</i>, cheap<i>est</i>. Indonesian never changes it. It puts a small word in front instead, and that is all.</p>
<h3>lebih: more, -er</h3>
<p><b>lebih</b> + describing word means "more …" or "…-er". To say "than", add <b>dari</b> or <b>daripada</b>; <b>daripada</b> is a little more careful, and both are correct.</p>
{{ex:Ini lebih murah.|EE-nee luh-BEEH MOO-rahh|This one is cheaper.}}
{{ex:Baju ini lebih murah dari baju itu.|BAH-joo EE-nee luh-BEEH MOO-rahh DAH-ree BAH-joo EE-too|This shirt is cheaper than that shirt.}}
{{ex:Jakarta lebih besar daripada Bandung.|jah-KAHR-tah luh-BEEH buh-SAHR dah-ree-PAH-dah BAHN-doong|Jakarta is bigger than Bandung.}}
<h3>paling: most, -est</h3>
<p><b>paling</b> + describing word means "the most …" or "the …-est". With a noun, link it with <b>yang</b>: <b>toko yang paling murah</b>, "the cheapest shop". Do not use <b>sangat</b> here: it only means "very".</p>
{{ex:Yang ini paling enak.|yahng EE-nee PAH-leeng EH-nahq|This one is the tastiest.}}
{{ex:Ini toko yang paling murah.|EE-nee TOH-koh yahng PAH-leeng MOO-rahh|This is the cheapest shop.}}
<h3>The same: sama … -nya, se-</h3>
<p>For "the same size" or "the same price", say <b>sama</b> + describing word + <b>-nya</b>. For "as … as", put <b>se-</b> on the describing word.</p>
{{ex:Dua tas ini sama besarnya.|DOO-ah tahs EE-nee SAH-mah buh-SAHR-nyah|These two bags are the same size.}}
{{ex:Adik saya setinggi saya.|AH-deeq SAH-yah suh-TEENG-ghee SAH-yah|My little brother is as tall as me.}}
<h3>kurang and terlalu</h3>
<p><b>kurang</b> means "less" or "not … enough". You already know it from bargaining: <b>Bisa kurang?</b> asks for less. <b>terlalu</b> means "too", as in "too expensive".</p>
{{ex:Kopinya kurang manis.|koh-PEE-nyah KOO-rahng MAH-nees|The coffee isn't sweet enough.}}
{{ex:Terlalu mahal, Bu. Bisa kurang?|tuhr-LAH-loo MAH-hahl boo BEE-sah KOO-rahng|It's too expensive, ma'am. Can you go lower?}}
<h3>Which one?</h3>
<p>To ask which of two things wins, start with <b>Mana yang</b> ("which one is…"): <b>Mana yang lebih murah?</b> The shopkeeper can answer with <b>yang ini</b> or <b>yang itu</b>. For "better", say <b>lebih baik</b>; for "the best", <b>paling baik</b> or, for food, <b>paling enak</b>.</p>
{{ex:Mana yang lebih murah?|MAH-nah yahng luh-BEEH MOO-rahh|Which one is cheaper?}}
<h3>Still no "to be"</h3>
<p>A comparison is a describing word with a helper in front, so the old rule holds: nothing stands for "is". <b>Baju ini lebih murah</b> is the whole sentence.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving out <b>lebih</b>, because "than" seems to be enough. <b>Ini murah dari itu</b> → <b>Ini lebih murah dari itu</b>.</div>
`,
