    title: "Plurals by repeating: buku-buku",
    blurb: "Know when Indonesian marks 'more than one', when to repeat a word, and when never to.",
    deck: "People and family",
    body: `
<p>English adds -s to almost every plural: book, books. Indonesian usually adds nothing at all. <b>buku</b> is "book" or "books", and the situation makes it clear.</p>
{{ex:Saya membeli buku.|SAH-yah muhm-buh-LEE BOO-koo|I bought a book. (Or: I bought books.)}}
<h3>Numbers and quantity words do the work</h3>
<p>When the number matters, say it: a number or a word such as <b>banyak</b> (many), <b>beberapa</b> (some, a few) or <b>semua</b> (all) goes before the noun, and the noun stays as it is.</p>
{{ex:Saya punya dua anak.|SAH-yah POO-nyah DOO-ah AH-nahq|I have two children.}}
{{ex:Ada banyak orang di pasar.|AH-dah BAH-nyahq OH-rahng dee PAH-sahr|There are a lot of people at the market.}}
<h3>Repeating the word</h3>
<p>To stress that there are several, or many different ones, repeat the noun with a hyphen: <b>anak-anak</b>, <b>buku-buku</b>. You will hear this often with people: <b>anak-anak</b> is the usual way to say "the kids".</p>
{{ex:Anak-anak sedang bermain di luar.|AH-nahq AH-nahq suh-DAHNG buhr-MAH-een dee LOO-ahr|The children are playing outside.}}
{{ex:Buku-buku saya di kamar.|BOO-koo BOO-koo SAH-yah dee KAH-mahr|My books are in my room.}}
<h3>Never after a number</h3>
<p>A number or a quantity word already says "more than one", so the noun is not repeated as well: <b>tiga buku</b>, <b>banyak orang</b>. English speakers often say both, because English marks the plural twice ("three books"). The pattern is always the same: the number or quantity word, then the plain noun, then any describing word: <b>dua kopi panas</b>.</p>
<h3>The hyphen is part of the spelling</h3>
<p>A repeated word is one word, written with a hyphen. In text messages you will see <b>anak2</b>; that is fine with friends, but not in careful writing or in this course's answers.</p>
<h3>Repeated words that are not plurals</h3>
<p>Some words only exist in repeated form, and some change meaning when repeated. These are not plurals at all.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td class="k">kupu-kupu</td><td>butterfly (one or more)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">hati-hati</td><td>be careful</td></tr><tr><td class="k">jalan-jalan</td><td>go out for fun, take a trip</td></tr><tr><td class="k">sama-sama</td><td>you're welcome</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Hati-hati di jalan!|HAH-tee HAH-tee dee JAH-lahn|Take care on the road!}}
{{ex:Kami mau jalan-jalan ke Ubud.|KAH-mee mow JAH-lahn JAH-lahn kuh OO-bood|We want to take a trip to Ubud.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> marking the plural twice. <b>tiga buku-buku</b> → <b>tiga buku</b>.</div>
`,
