    id: "casual",
    title: "Everyday spoken Indonesian",
    blurb: "Understand the casual Indonesian you hear every day, and know when to use it and when not to.",
    deck: "Everyday spoken Indonesian",
    body: `
<p>This course has taught standard, polite Indonesian, which works with everyone. Among friends, on the street and online, you will hear shorter everyday forms. Learn to understand them first, then use them with friends your own age.</p>
<h3>Short forms of words you know</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Casual</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">tidak</td><td class="k">nggak, enggak</td><td>not, no</td></tr><tr><td class="k">sudah</td><td class="k">udah</td><td>already</td></tr><tr><td class="k">saja</td><td class="k">aja</td><td>just, only</td></tr><tr><td class="k">bagaimana</td><td class="k">gimana</td><td>how</td></tr><tr><td class="k">mengapa</td><td class="k">kenapa</td><td>why</td></tr><tr><td class="k">saya</td><td class="k">aku</td><td>I, me</td></tr><tr><td class="k">terima kasih</td><td class="k">makasih</td><td>thanks</td></tr><tr><td class="k">sangat, sekali</td><td class="k">banget</td><td>very (after the describing word)</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Aku nggak tahu.|AH-koo nggahq TAH-hoo|I don't know.}}
{{ex:Udah makan?|OO-dahh MAH-kahn|Have you eaten yet?}}
{{ex:Gimana kabarnya?|ghee-MAH-nah kah-BAHR-nyah|How's it going?}}
<h3>Verbs get shorter too</h3>
<p>In casual speech the root often replaces the <b>meN-</b> verb (<b>Aku baca buku</b>), and the ending <b>-in</b> replaces <b>-kan</b> or <b>-i</b>: <b>bilangin</b> means "tell (someone)".</p>
{{ex:Nanti aku bilangin dia.|NAHN-tee AH-koo bee-LAH-ngeen DEE-ah|I'll tell him later.}}
<h3>Tone words: dong, sih, kok, deh</h3>
<p>These small words add feeling, the way English uses tone of voice. <b>dong</b> pushes ("come on"), <b>sih</b> softens or adds "anyway", <b>kok</b> shows surprise ("how come?"), and <b>deh</b> settles things ("OK then").</p>
{{ex:Kok mahal banget?|kohq MAH-hahl BAH-nguht|How come it's so expensive?}}
{{ex:Ya udah, aku pulang deh.|yah OO-dahh AH-koo POO-lahng dehh|OK then, I'll head home.}}
<h3>gue and lo</h3>
<p>In Jakarta, friends say <b>gue</b> for "I" and <b>lo</b> for "you". Use them only with Jakarta friends your own age, never with strangers or older people. Learn to recognize them; you do not need to say them. Outside Jakarta they can sound out of place, and other regions have their own casual words.</p>
<h3>When to switch</h3>
<p>Use standard Indonesian with strangers, older people, at work and in writing to a teacher, landlord or office. Casual forms are for friends, people your age and children. If someone older or a stranger switches to casual with you, you can follow them, but let them go first. Even in casual speech the noun still comes first and there is still no word for "is": <b>Rumahku jauh</b>.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using friends' words with an older person. <b>Pak, gue nggak bisa datang</b> → <b>Pak, saya tidak bisa datang</b>.</div>
`,
